  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nHaving a mandatory `value` attribute on `<data>` elements but _never_ a `name` feels unintuitive. Sure, the `value` attribute is technically enough to designate the human-readable content, but functionally and semantically it can become clumsy.\n\nMost often, the machine-readable data is natively expressed as a key-value pair, which needs to be reflected in the mark-up. Sure, we can always do `data-name=\"itemName\"` + `value=\"itemValue\"` or simply `value=\"itemName=itemValue\"`, but `<data name=\"itemName\" value=\"itemValue>item</data>` would make so much more sense.\n\nThis would be useful to query a database in a REST API for the corresponding object in a straightforward manner, which is likely one of the most prominent use-cases for the <data> element itself. Therefore, the proposition is to allow `name` as an optional attribute for `<data>`.\n\n\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\n_No response_\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\n_No response_\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
